# Angular 22: What's New and Why It Matters

Angular 22 has arrived, consolidating an era of transformation that began back in Angular 16 with the experimental introduction of Signals. If you've been following the framework's evolution, you'll notice this release isn't just another numeric increment — it's the maturity point of a philosophical rewrite of Angular.

In this article, I'll detail each relevant feature with code examples and analysis of the real-world impact on day-to-day development.

---

## 1. Zoneless by Default (No Zone.js)

This is, without a doubt, the biggest milestone of this version. Angular 22 is the first release where **new applications generated by the CLI are already zoneless by default**.

For over a decade, Zone.js was the "magic" behind Angular's automatic change detection. It intercepts async APIs like `setTimeout`, Promises, and DOM events to notify Angular when to update the view. The problem? It's expensive, hard to debug, and incompatible with some modern APIs.

With Signals and the new `effect()`, Angular has its own dependency tracking mechanism. Zone.js is no longer necessary.

```typescript
// Angular 22 - app.config.ts generated by the CLI
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideRouter } from '@angular/router';
import { provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { routes } from './app.routes';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideRouter(routes),
    provideHttpClient()
    // No provideZoneChangeDetection() — zoneless is the default!
  ]
};
```

The performance impact is real: smaller bundles (~40KB less), faster startup, and elimination of monkey-patching on native APIs.

---

## 2. Signals: Stable and Complete API

Angular 22 elevates the entire Signals API to stable status. This includes features that were in developer preview:

### `linkedSignal` — Derivation with Its Own State

The `linkedSignal` allows creating a signal that derives from another but can also be modified manually:

```typescript
import { signal, linkedSignal } from '@angular/core';

const items = signal(['Angular', 'React', 'Vue']);

// Derives from the parent signal, but has its own state
const selected = linkedSignal({
  source: items,
  computation: (items, prev) => {
    // Keeps selection if it still exists in the new array
    if (prev && items.includes(prev.value)) return prev.value;
    return items[0]; // Otherwise, select the first
  }
});

console.log(selected()); // 'Angular'
selected.set('React');    // Manual modification
console.log(selected()); // 'React'

items.set(['Next.js', 'Nuxt', 'Angular']);
console.log(selected()); // 'Angular' — reacted because 'React' no longer exists
```

### `resource()` — Async Data with Signals

The `resource()` is Angular's answer to the async data fetching problem with Signals. Think of it as a `computed` that can make HTTP requests:

```typescript
import { signal, resource } from '@angular/core';
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({ ... })
export class UserProfileComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  userId = signal(1);

  userResource = resource({
    request: () => ({ id: this.userId() }),
    loader: ({ request }) =>
      this.http.get<User>(`/api/users/${request.id}`)
  });

  // Reactive access
  user = this.userResource.value;       // Signal<User | undefined>
  isLoading = this.userResource.isLoading; // Signal<boolean>
  error = this.userResource.error;      // Signal<unknown>
}
```

---

## 3. Hot Module Replacement (HMR) with Preserved State

Before Angular 22, HMR already existed, but when modifying a component, the application state was lost — the page restarted from scratch.

Now, with **Stateful HMR**, Angular preserves Signal states between reloads. This is a huge productivity gain:

```typescript
@Component({ ... })
export class TodosComponent {
  items = signal<Todo[]>([]);
  filter = signal<'all' | 'done' | 'pending'>('all');

  // When editing the template, these signals maintain their state!
  // No more manually reproducing state to test a visual change.
}
```

---

## 4. Incremental Hydration — Stable

Incremental Hydration (introduced as experimental in Angular 17) is now **fully stable** in Angular 22.

The idea is simple but powerful: in SSR applications, instead of hydrating the entire document at once (blocking the main thread), you declare **when** each part of the page should be hydrated.

```typescript
// app.config.ts with SSR
import { provideClientHydration, withIncrementalHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideClientHydration(withIncrementalHydration())
  ]
};
```

In the template, use `@defer` with hydration triggers:

```html
<!-- Hydrates only when the component enters the viewport -->
@defer (hydrate on viewport) {
  <app-comments-section />
}

<!-- Hydrates only when the user interacts -->
@defer (hydrate on interaction) {
  <app-rating-widget />
}

<!-- Never hydrates — purely static content -->
@defer (hydrate never) {
  <app-static-footer />
}
```

The impact on Core Web Vitals is direct: **TTI (Time to Interactive) drops dramatically** because you only hydrate what needs to be interactive.

---

## 5. Migrating: Is It Worth Updating?

**Yes, and the CLI makes it easy.** Angular offers automatic migration schematics:

```bash
# Update to Angular 22
ng update @angular/core@22 @angular/cli@22

# Migrate input decorators to the new function API
ng generate @angular/core:signal-input-migration

# Migrate outputs to the new API
ng generate @angular/core:output-migration
```

---

## Conclusion

Angular 22 isn't a revolution — it's the **consolidation of a revolution**. Every feature in this version existed in some previous form, but the combination represents an Angular that would be unrecognizable to a developer who stopped using the framework in 2020.

Faster, more predictable, more typesafe. And, finally, without Zone.js.

---

*Ghabryel Henrique is a Senior Software Engineer specializing in Angular. Follow me on [Medium](https://ghabryel.medium.com) or contact me on [LinkedIn](https://linkedin.com/in/ghabryelhenrique).*
